{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786428973,"nonce":"mMihIdJRf++A4nrLeq0853sRpM8=","message":"Fixed with a batch endpoint under rules/api (ADR 0025). GET /rule-snapshots/batch takes snapshot ids, one `id = ANY($2)`, chunked at 100 and deduped; the alert list derives the sorted distinct rule.id set across loaded pages and fetches once with staleTime: Infinity, since snapshots are immutable. A new Rule column renders truncated mono expr, with for and an ambiguous match confidence in the tooltip.\n\nThe embedded-DTO option was rejected on ownership, not payload: it needs either alerts/api importing rules/domain (a straight §5.4 breach) or a hand-maintained fifteen-field copy of a rules-owned schema living under alerts, so 'what did the rule say' gets two answers that drift. The stated cost of the batch — two requests, a client-side join, no atomicity — is real and is written down in the ADR; the mitigations are that the join key is a UUID and the joined rows are immutable.\n\nTwo things that made it clearly right rather than merely acceptable: rules/api ALREADY registers /alerts/{id}/rule reaching alerts through an AlertReader port it declares itself, so the sanctioned pattern existed; and because snapshots are content-addressed, a page of 50 alerts under an unchanged rule is ONE id, not 50.\n\nThe cell distinguishes four absences (no snapshot bound / resolving / resolved to nothing / captured as unavailable) instead of rendering an empty cell for all four, and the column is not drawn at all when the caller did not ask — so a column of dashes can never claim 'oto captured no rule' when the truth is 'nobody asked'.\n\nReported, not fixed: Router.embed calls svc.Get once per alert row — a pre-existing server-side N+1 that also affects include=current_occurrence, making the contract's 'batch-loaded' claim untrue.","files":null}]}